  "Physician, short-story writer and novelist; the author of The Walls of Jericho and the first Black writer of the Harlem detective story.",
  "Born Rudolph John Chauncey Fisher in Washington, D.C., on 9 May 1897; he studied medicine at Howard and Columbia, worked as a radiologist in New York, and published The Walls of Jericho (1928), The Conjure-Man Dies (1932, the first Black detective novel) and the stories of The City of Refuge. He died in New York on 26 December 1934.",
  "Rudolph Fisher was the first Black writer of the Harlem detective story and one of the finest novelists of the Renaissance: The Walls of Jericho made the Black city its subject, and The Conjure-Man Dies created the Black detective novel and made Harlem the setting of a new kind of American crime fiction.",
  "The comic novel of Harlem; the detective story with a Black detective; the exact dialect of the city; the medical detail and the autopsy; the plain, witty, rapid prose.",
  "Race, the colour line, and the New Negro; the American city, the machine, and the moneyed metropolis; the Depression, unemployment, and the hunger march; class, the servant problem, and the new democracy.",
  "Rudolph Fisher was the first Black writer of the Harlem detective story: The Walls of Jericho made the Black city its subject, and The Conjure-Man Dies created the Black detective novel and made Harlem the setting of a new kind of American crime fiction.",
